{
  "description": "Opt-in cancel-all loop (TS cancelAll({symbol, untilDone: true}), Python cancel_all(symbol, until_done=True), Go CancelAllUntilDone). Each case feeds the SDK a sequence of 200 responses from POST /api/v1/trading/orders/cancel-all (the `data` object only), in order. The runner uses a fake clock: calls take no time; the only time that passes is the loop's own sleeps. Rules: repeat while has_more is true or any failure code is INVALID_STATE or SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE. After a round with no progress (cancelled + already_closed empty), sleep 1, 2, 4, 8, then 15 s (cap) before the next call; reset after any progress; no sleep after a round with progress. Stop when done, after max_rounds calls (default 20), or when the next sleep would bring the elapsed time to or past time_budget_s (default 120). The result merges rounds by order_id; an order's latest state wins (a later cancelled/already_closed removes an earlier failure). Other failure codes are returned, never a reason to continue. Order-id lists compare as sets; `stopped` is done | max_rounds | time_budget; `failure_codes` maps each still-failed order to its code. Inside the loop the transport does NOT retry (max retries 0): every HTTP request is one round, so the loop never sends more than max_rounds requests. A response item with `http_status` is an error response (`error` is the error body, `headers` its headers). A retryable error (429, a retryable 5xx, a network failure) counts as a round without progress: after a 429 the loop waits Retry-After / retry_after_seconds (exactly, no jitter) instead of the backoff step and does not advance the backoff; after any other retryable error it waits the next backoff step. A wait that would bring the elapsed time to or past the budget is not taken: the loop stops with stopped=time_budget and last_error_code set. A non-retryable error ends the loop by raising it, with the partial result attached (expect.error_code, expect.partial_cancelled).",
